# Pull request branch workflow

## Request-time default (binding)

**Unless the user explicitly directs otherwise in the same message** that
assigns the work (examples: “push straight to `main`”, “no PR”, “hotfix to
default branch only”), **assume all work is to be completed on a pull
request:** dedicated branch off fresh `origin/main` → commits → push → open
or update a **PR** into `main` (draft is acceptable). Silence or omission is
**not** permission to bypass a PR.

**Cloud / background agents:** “commit and push when done” means push the
**PR branch** and open a PR — **never** interpret it as permission to push
`origin/main`.

## Speed — nothing held back from `main`

This workflow must **not** add latency versus direct commits to `main`:

- **Integration path is unchanged:** code reaches `main` when the **PR is
  merged** (squash merge, merge commit, or rebase-and-merge). That is
  production delivery — not a separate approval step before work starts.
- **Guards target mistakes, not merges:** `ci-main-push-gate.yml` runs on
  **every push to `main`** (no paths filter). It allows squash/merge commits,
  PR-linked commits (GitHub API), and `[direct-main]`. Feature-branch PR CI
  only checks head ref ≠ `main`/`master`.
- **Do not ask permission to start** normal scoped work. Branch creation +
  PR is mechanical (~seconds); proceed immediately unless an exception below
  applies.
- **Do not leave work “almost on main”:** when implementation is done, the
  agent must state **PR URL**, **merge readiness** (CI green / known gaps),
  and that **merging the PR ships to `main`** (and triggers deploy paths that
  watch `main`). Never end with only a branch push and no PR.

## Communication — when the user must be involved

Use a **short, explicit** message only in these cases (not for routine work):

| Situation | Agent action |
|-----------|----------------|
| Normal scoped work | No pre-approval. Branch → implement → push → **open/update PR** → tell user **“Merge PR #N to ship to `main`.”** |
| Work finished, PR open | Same: PR link + merge instruction. Do **not** ask “may I start?” |
| User authorized direct `main` in **this** message | Commit on `main` with `[direct-main]`; say what landed and that deploy may follow. |
| **Emergency / prod down / security hotfix** and user did **not** authorize direct `main` | **Stop before pushing `main`.** Send the **escalation block** below; offer **merge existing PR** if one exists, else **direct-main** only after user replies in-thread. |
| Cannot open PR (tooling / permissions) | Push branch; give **exact** `gh pr create` (or GitHub UI) steps + branch name. Label **BLOCKED: needs you to open PR** so nothing is assumed merged. |
| `main-push-gate` or branch protection failed on `main` | Report failing commit SHA + check name; say whether **merging the PR** fixes it vs user must **`[direct-main]`** / bypass. |

### Escalation block (copy when user input is required)

```
INTEGRATION — needs your call (<one-line reason, e.g. prod incident>)

• Intended fix: <summary>
• Branch / PR: <branch> | PR #<n> <url> — OR — no PR yet
• Fastest path to main: (A) Merge PR #n  (B) You reply “push to main” and I use [direct-main]
• Risk if we wait: <one line>
• What I need from you: reply A or B (or “push to main” for B)
```

Do **not** use vague “let me know if you want a PR.” Either merge is ready, or the
block above applies.

## Ordered integration strategy (before any file edit)

Follow the **first applicable** path:

### 1. PREFERRED — environment already provides a non-main branch

If the coding environment (GitHub Copilot, Cursor Cloud, CI) supplies a
pre-created, non-main branch:

1. Confirm you are on that branch (`git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD`).
2. `git fetch origin` and verify the branch is based on current `origin/main`.
3. Use it directly — **do not** attempt to create a second branch.
4. Activate reuse mode if running the helper: `bash scripts/git/prepare-work-branch.sh <topic> --reuse`

### 2. NORMAL — create a dedicated PR branch

If no branch was pre-assigned and ref creation is permitted:

1. `git fetch origin`
2. `bash scripts/git/prepare-work-branch.sh <topic-slug>` (creates `cursor/<topic>` from `origin/main`)
3. State the branch name in the first reply for the scoped request.

Do **not** stage, commit, or push until step 2 is done.

### 3. RESTRICTED-REF ENVIRONMENT — GitHub returns GH013

If `git push` fails with `GH013: Cannot create ref due to creations being restricted`:

1. **Stop retrying** — further push attempts will also fail.
2. Check whether the task provides an existing writable branch (Path 1 above).
3. If one exists, use it.
4. If none exists, **report the block deterministically**:

```
BLOCKED_GITHUB_REF_CREATION
attempted_branch=<name>
base=origin/main (SHA: <sha>)
error=GH013: Cannot create ref due to creations being restricted

Resolution — choose one:
  (A) Ask a maintainer to pre-create branch '<name>' from main and assign Copilot to it.
  (B) Ask a repo admin to allow creation of refs matching copilot/** or cursor/** in the repository ruleset.
  (C) You (the user) reply "[direct-main]" in this thread to authorize direct-main for this governance task.
```

5. **Do not fabricate a PR** or claim work is integrated when only a local branch exists.

### 4. EXPLICIT DIRECT-MAIN (same-request exception only)

Only when the user explicitly states direct-main permission **in the current message**:

- Include `[direct-main]` in every commit message on `main`.
- The CI gate (`assert-main-push-authorized.sh`) allows it.
- Direct-main is an **exception**, not the new default.

## Why this rule exists

Shipping several commits directly to the default branch (`main`) bypasses
GitHub’s review surface, hides the change set behind unrelated history, and
makes it easy to miss CI on the intended diff. **Rule 15** (doc / PR / code
parity) assumes a PR exists for final-body checks — it does **not** replace
this workflow rule.

## Rule — use a PR branch for new work

1. **Scoped work:** For any **new scoped request** (feature, WO, wave, bugfix,
   refactor), do all implementation commits on a **dedicated branch** that will
   back a **pull request** into the repo default branch (here: `main`), per the
   request-time default above.
2. **Branch off current remote default:** Before the first commit,
   `git fetch origin` and create the branch from **`origin/main`** (or the
   user-specified base), not from a stale local `main`.
3. **Naming:** Follow repo convention — e.g. `cursor/<topic>`,
   `feat/<topic>`, `fix/<topic>` — consistent with sibling branches.
4. **Push + PR:** Push the branch and **open the PR** (draft is fine) as soon
   as there is a meaningful first commit, unless the environment cannot create
   PRs — then leave explicit instructions for the human to open one.

## Rule — before every commit on a PR branch

Confirm the branch is still **appropriate to receive new commits**:

1. **`git fetch origin`** (refresh refs).
2. **If a PR already exists for this branch:** confirm it is **open** and
   **not merged** (e.g. `gh pr view --state`, GitHub UI, or API). If the PR is
   **merged** or **closed**, **do not** add further commits on that branch for
   new work — open a **new** branch from current `origin/main` instead.
3. **If default branch absorbed the work without a PR:** treat prior branch as
   read-only; start a **new** branch for any follow-up.

### Merge detection without `gh`

If `origin/main` can reach this branch’s tip and the tip is already on
`origin/main`’s history as the merge result of the PR, the branch is done —
verify with `git branch -r --merged origin/main` or compare PR state on GitHub.

## Technical enforcement (repo)

| Mechanism | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| `scripts/git/prepare-work-branch.sh` | Create PR branch from `origin/main` |
| `scripts/git/assert-not-on-main-branch.sh` | Fail commits on `main`/`master` |
| `scripts/git/install-pre-commit-hook.sh` | Optional local pre-commit guard |
| `scripts/ci/assert-main-push-authorized.sh` | CI fails unauthorized `main` tips |
| `docs/RUNBOOKS/github-branch-protection.md` | Enable required checks on `main` |

**Direct-main override (same-request only):** `ALLOW_DIRECT_MAIN_PUSH=1` for
local commit, and **`[direct-main]`** in the commit message for pushes that
land on `main`. Do not use overrides without explicit user direction in the
current message.

## Exceptions

- The user **states in the same request** that work may bypass a PR (e.g.
  direct push to `main`), or **governance override** (`docs/governance.md`)
  directs commits to `main` / another integration branch. Log material
  governance exceptions when applicable.

## Pre-commit check

- [ ] Unless the user directed otherwise **in this request**, did I run
      `prepare-work-branch.sh` (or equivalent) **before the first edit**?
- [ ] Am I on a **non-default** branch created from fresh `origin/main` (or
      declared base)?
- [ ] If continuing prior work on a named branch: is its PR **still open** (or
      no PR yet and work is still part of the same scoped request)?
- [ ] Have I pushed and opened (or instructed opening) a **PR** for this
      branch, except when an explicit same-request or governance exception
      applies?
- [ ] Did I avoid `git push origin main` unless the user authorized direct-main
      in **this** message?
